# `.to(Type)` must reach the universal conversion, not just the trait default.
#
# `make Showable do let to(String)` is a type-directed trait DEFAULT: it answers
# for `String` and nothing else. The interface advertises it as the backend
# entry point (`to/Showable`), so a call site binds to it DIRECTLY and never
# consults the generated `to/2` dispatcher — the only thing that knows about
# the generic `to$generic`. So on BEAM every non-String conversion reached the
# default's `String`-only clause and raised `function_clause`, while the walker
# fell through to the universal `to`. It took `spec/fact.kex` and four others
# down with it, all through `args.first.or("50").to(Integer)`.
#
# The shape matters: a conversion used INLINE (interpolated straight into a
# string) lowered to the dispatcher and worked, so this only reproduced through
# a binding. Both spellings are here — the inline ones would have passed on
# their own.
#
# NOT pinned here, because the backends still disagree: `badge.to(String)` on a
# record with its own `showValue` gives `Just(badge:gold)` on the walker and
# `Just(Badge { kind: gold })` on BEAM. That is the mirror image of the bug
# above — the call site binds to the DISPATCHER, whose generic arm renders the
# record instead of reaching the trait default that would have called the
# type's `showValue`. Pre-existing, and only observable once the crash above
# was out of the way. `badge.showValue` and `IO.printLine(badge)` agree on both
# backends, so it is specific to the conversion path.

record Badge do
  kind : String
end

make Badge, implement: Showable do
  let showValue -> String = "badge:${this.kind}"
end

main do
  # Bound conversions — each of these raised function_clause on BEAM.
  let n = "50".to(Integer)
  let f = "50".to(Float)
  let s = 42.to(String)
  let bad = "nope".to(Integer)
  IO.printLine("${n} ${f} ${s} ${bad}")

  # Inline, for contrast: these took the dispatcher and always worked.
  IO.printLine("${"7".to(Integer)} ${7.to(Float)}")

  # A conversion with no answer is None, not a crash — this is the clause that
  # used to raise, reached through a receiver that implements Showable.
  let badge = Badge { kind: "gold" }
  IO.printLine("${badge.to(Integer)}")
  IO.printLine("${badge.showValue}")
end
